When a small group of living organisms (small population) drift away from the main population from which it originates (for example, if it migrates through a mountainous chain or river or migrates to a new island and it is difficult to return), it will often find itself in a different environment than it previously lived.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population will need different properties or adaptations so that it can cope with them.
The source population has not in any way changed, they still need the same adjustments as before.
When the new population begins to adapt to a new environment, over and over time it is less and less similar to the other population.
Over time, in several thousand or even millions of years, the populations will look so different that they can not be considered the same species.
This process is called speciation, which means the formation of new species. Specification is an unavoidable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
